This is a good candidate for parallelization. The Smith-Waterman algorithm has a computationally intensive matrix construction phase that can be optimized.

I will follow my plan: PLAN → PATCH → TEST → FINALIZE.
PLAN

    Analysis: The core of the work is in the constructMatrix method. It builds a scoring matrix where each cell H[i][j] depends on its top H[i-1][j], left H[i][j-1], and top-left H[i-1][j-1] neighbors. This dependency prevents simple row- or column-wise parallelization. The findHighestScore and traceback phases are less intensive but will be reviewed. The traceback is inherently sequential.

    Strategy: The dependency structure of constructMatrix is a classic fit for a wavefront (or anti-diagonal) parallelization pattern.
        Instead of iterating row-by-row, the computation will proceed along anti-diagonals.
        All cells on a given anti-diagonal (where i + j = k) can be computed in parallel because they only depend on cells from previous anti-diagonals (k-1 and k-2), which are already computed.
        I will use Java's parallel streams (IntStream.range().parallel().forEach()) to compute the cells within each anti-diagonal concurrently.
        A sequential fallback will be implemented for small matrices where parallel overhead would be detrimental.

    Alternatives Considered:
        Parallelizing only findHighestScore: This is simpler but targets a much smaller part of the overall computation, yielding minimal performance gains. The matrix construction is the true bottleneck.
        Blocking: Dividing the matrix into blocks is another valid strategy, but it's more complex to manage the dependencies at the block boundaries compared to the more natural fit of the wavefront approach for this specific problem.

