Okay, I will begin by planning the parallelization of the provided Smith-Waterman algorithm.

PLAN

    Analysis: The core of the algorithm is the constructMatrix method, which populates a 2D matrix H. The calculation of each cell H[i][j] depends on its top H[i-1][j], left H[i][j-1], and top-left H[i-1][j-1] neighbors. This creates a loop-carried dependency, making simple parallelization of the i or j loops incorrect.

    Strategy: The dependency structure allows for a "wavefront" or "anti-diagonal" parallelization strategy. All cells along an anti-diagonal k = i + j can be computed in parallel because they only depend on cells from previous anti-diagonals (k-1 and k-2). I will use a Java ForkJoinPool to manage a pool of worker threads that will compute the cells of each anti-diagonal in parallel. The main thread will iterate through the anti-diagonals sequentially, ensuring dependencies are met, but the work within each anti-diagonal is done in parallel. The findHighestScore and traceback methods have dependencies that make them inherently sequential and will not be parallelized.

    Alternatives Considered:
        Row/Column Parallelism: This is incorrect because each row/column depends on the previous one, preventing simultaneous computation.
        Blocking: Dividing the matrix into blocks can work but adds significant complexity to manage dependencies at block boundaries. The wavefront approach is more direct for this specific dependency pattern.

