Here's my plan.

1. Analysis and Strategy

   Dependency: The core of the Smith-Waterman algorithm is filling the scoring matrix H. Each cell H[i][j] depends on its top H[i-1][j], left H[i][j-1], and top-left H[i-1][j-1] neighbors. This creates a "wavefront" data dependency, meaning a simple parallelization of the i or j loops would be incorrect and produce a race condition.
   Parallel Strategy: The correct way to parallelize this is to compute the matrix along its anti-diagonals. All cells (i, j) where i + j = k for a constant k are independent of each other and can be computed in parallel. We process these anti-diagonals sequentially, from k=2 up to k = (n-1) + (m-1).
   Implementation: I will use a ForkJoinPool with a fixed number of threads (matching the CPU core count) to manage parallelism. For each anti-diagonal, an IntStream.range().parallel() will be used to compute the cell values. This task will be submitted to the custom ForkJoinPool to ensure resource bounds are respected.
   Rejected Alternatives:
   Naive Parallel Loops: Applying parallelStream() directly to the existing i or j loops is incorrect due to the loop-carried dependency.
   Blocking: Dividing the matrix into blocks is another valid strategy, but it's more complex to implement correctly due to dependencies at block edges. The anti-diagonal approach is simpler and sufficient here.

2. Scope of Changes

   A new file SmithWatermanParallel.java will be created for the parallel implementation.
   The constructMatrix method will be rewritten to use the anti-diagonal approach with a fixed-thread ForkJoinPool.
   The findHighestScore and traceback methods are much less computationally intensive and will remain sequential to keep the changes minimal and focused on the primary bottleneck.

This strategy guarantees deterministic results because the order of computation for the anti-diagonals is fixed, and within each diagonal, the calculations are independent.

