I will start by creating a parallel version of the SmithWaterman algorithm. The key challenge is the data dependency in the constructMatrix method, where each cell depends on its top, left, and top-left neighbors. A standard parallel for loop on rows or columns will not work.

I will use a wavefront parallelization strategy based on anti-diagonals. All cells on an anti-diagonal (i, j) where i + j = k are independent of each other and only depend on cells from previous anti-diagonals (k-1, k-2). This allows me to process the cells of a single anti-diagonal in parallel.

The implementation will iterate sequentially through the anti-diagonals, and for each one, it will use a parallel stream to compute the cell values. This maintains correctness while introducing parallelism. I will also include a threshold to fall back to the sequential version for small inputs, where parallel overhead would dominate.

I'll create a new file SmithWatermanParallel.java for this implementation.

