The performance test shows a significant regression. The parallel version is much slower than the sequential one. This is a classic case of parallelization overhead overwhelming the actual work. Submitting a task to the ForkJoinPool for each anti-diagonal and then immediately joining on it creates a huge amount of overhead and effectively serializes the outer loop, defeating the purpose of parallelism. The work done on each anti-diagonal is too small to justify this approach.

I will refine the implementation to use a more suitable synchronization mechanism that avoids the high overhead of task submission for each step. A CyclicBarrier is a good fit here. I will create a pool of worker threads once, and they will synchronize at the end of computing each anti-diagonal using the barrier. This way, the threads are long-lived, and the synchronization cost is much lower.

Refinement Plan:

    Modify SmithWatermanParallel.java:
        Replace the ForkJoinPool with a fixed-size ExecutorService and a CyclicBarrier.
        The worker threads will be created once and will loop through the anti-diagonals.
        In each iteration, each thread will compute a range of cells for the current anti-diagonal.
        After computing its portion, each thread will wait on the CyclicBarrier. This ensures all threads have finished the current anti-diagonal before proceeding to the next, thus respecting the wavefront dependency.
    Re-run Tests: After patching the file, I will recompile and run both the correctness and performance tests to ensure the new implementation is correct, deterministic, and provides a speedup.

